<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-s10e01" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Episode 1: Solve Systems of Equations by Graphing</title>
  <assemblage>
    <title>Episode 1 Learning Goals</title>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Interpret a system of linear equations, and interpret a solution to a system 
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Solve a system of linear equations by graphing both equations and looking for the point of intersection 
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </assemblage>
  <assemblage>
    <title>Episode 1 Key Terminology</title>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            System of equations
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Solution to a system of equations
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </assemblage>
  <assemblage>
    <title>Episode 1 Key Symbols</title>
    <p>
      <md>
        \left\{ \right. \quad\quad \text{as in} \,\, \left\{ \begin{array}{l} x+ y  = 1\\ 2x-y = 2 \end{array}\right.
      </md>
    </p>
  </assemblage>
  <definition xml:id="def-system">
    <statement>
      <p>
        A <term>system of equations</term> consists of two or more equations that need to be true simultaneously.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  <p>
    Equations can be collected into a system by enclosing them with a left curly bracket, for example:
    
       <md> \left\{ \begin{array}{l} x+ y  = 1\\ 2x-y  = 2 \end{array}\right.</md>
      
      Remember that a single equation can be used to express a desired result or a known fact. 
      So a system of equations can be used to represent multiple desired results, or multiple known facts. 
  </p>
  <example>
    <p>
      A rectangle has an unknown length L and a width W. 
      You want to make a rectangle with a perimeter of 20 inches and an area of 25 square inches. Express this situation using a system of equations.
    </p>
    <p>
      Solution: First, you want the perimeter to be 20 inches. Using the perimeter formula for a rectangle, this becomes the equation:
      <md>
        2L+2W=20
      </md>
      Second, you want the area to be 25 square inches. Using the area formula for a rectangle, this becomes the equation:
      <md>
        LW = 25
      </md>
      Because I want both of these things to be true at the same time, I write both equations in a system:
      <md>
        \left\{ \begin{array}{l} 2L + 2W  = 20\\ LW  = 25 \end{array}\right.
      </md>
      A solution to a single equation is something that makes the equation true. It tells you how to achieve the result that the equation represents.
    </p>
    <p>
      A solution to a system of equations is something that makes ALL the equations true at the same time. 
      It tells you how to simultaneously achieve all the results that the equations represent. 
    </p>
  </example>
  <example>
    <p>
      Verify that <m>L=5, W =5</m> represents a solution to the system
      <md>
        \left\{ \begin{array}{l} 2L + 2W  = 20\\ LW  = 25 \end{array}\right.
      </md>
    </p>
    <p>
      I need to be able to plug <m>L=5</m> and <m>W=5</m> into BOTH equations and make BOTH equations true. Plugging the values into the first equation:
      <md>
        <mrow>2(5) + 2(5) \amp = 20 ? </mrow>
        <mrow>10+10 \amp = 20 ? </mrow>
        <mrow>20 \amp = 20 ? </mrow>
      </md>
      This is true, so <m>L=5</m> and <m>W=5</m> is a solution to the first equation.
    </p>
    <p>
      Let’s plug the values into the equation:
      <md>
        <mrow>(5)(5) \amp = 25?</mrow>
        <mrow>25 \amp = 25?</mrow>
      </md>
      This is true, so <m>L=5</m> and <m>W=5</m> is also a solution to the second equation.
    </p>
    <p>
      Because <m>L=5</m>, <m>W=5</m> was a solution to both equations, it is a solution to the system of equations.
    </p>
    <p>
      In other words, to make a rectangle that has a perimeter of 20 inches and an area of 25 inches, 
      you should make a rectangle that has a length of 5 inches and a width of 5 inches.
    </p>
  </example>
  <p>
    Remember that the points on the graph of an equation correspond to the solutions to that equation. 
    So, if a point is on the graph of two equations, it must be a solution to both equations.
  </p>
  <p>
    <alert>How to Solve a System of Equations by Graphing:</alert>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>
          Graph the equations on the system. Make sure to draw both graphs on the same xy-plane.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Look for points where the graphs intersect (points on the graph of both equations). 
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          The point of intersection is the solution to the system.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </p>
  <example>
    <p>
      Solve the system of equations:
      <md>
        \left\{ \begin{array}{l} y = -2x+1\\ y  = x-2 \end{array}\right.
      </md>
    </p>
    <p>
      Solution: We start by graphing both equations. 
    </p>
    <p>
      The first equation, <m>y=-2x+1</m>, has a slope of <m>-2</m> and a y-intercept of <m>(0,1)</m>. We use the techniques of Season 09 to quickly graph this equation.
    </p>
    <image width="60%">
      <shortdescription>a graph of a y = -2x + 1</shortdescription>
        <prefigure label="pf-101ex1a" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
        <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="5">
        <definition>f(x) = -2*x+1     
        </definition>
        <coordinates bbox="(-6,-6,6,6)">
        <grid-axes arrows="2" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"
        hticks="(-5,1,5)" vticks="(-5,1,5)" hlabels="(-5,1,5)" vlabels="(-5,1,5)"/>
             
                  <graph function = "f" stroke = "red"/>
        </coordinates>
        </diagram>
        </prefigure>
        </image>
        <p>
          The second equation, <m>y=x-2</m>, has a slope of <m>-2</m> and a y-intercept of <m>(0,-2)</m>. 
          We use the techniques of Season 09 to quickly graph this equation too.
        </p>
        <image width="60%">
      <shortdescription>a graph of a y = -2x + 1 and y = x-2</shortdescription>
        <prefigure label="pf-101ex1b" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
        <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="5">
        <definition>f(x) = -2*x+1 </definition>
        <definition>g(x) = x-2 </definition>
        <coordinates bbox="(-6,-6,6,6)">
        <grid-axes arrows="2" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"
        hticks="(-5,1,5)" vticks="(-5,1,5)" hlabels="(-5,1,5)" vlabels="(-5,1,5)"/>
             <graph function = "g" stroke = "blue"/>
              <graph function = "f" stroke = "red"/>
              <point p="(1,-1)" stroke="black" fill="black"/>
                <label anchor="(1,-1)" alignment="se">
                <m>(1, -1)</m>
                </label>
        </coordinates>
        </diagram>
        </prefigure>
        </image>
        <p>
          Notice that the point <m>(x,y)=(1,-1)</m> lies on both lines; it is the point where the two lines intersect. So, <m>x= 1, y=-1</m> is the solution to the system.
        </p>
  </example>
  <example>
    <p>
      Solve the system of equations:
      <md>
        \left\{ \begin{array}{l} x+y  = 2\\ x-2y  =4 \end{array}\right.
      </md>
    </p>
    <p>
      Solution: We start by graphing both equations. 
    </p>
    <p>
      The first equation is not currently written in slope-intercept form, so we start by solving for <m>y</m>.
      <md>
        <mrow>x+y \amp = 2 </mrow>
        <mrow>x+y-x \amp = 2-x </mrow>
         <mrow>y \amp = 2-x </mrow>
         <mrow>y \amp = -x+2 </mrow>
      </md>
      Now we can see the first equation has a slope of <m>m=-1</m> and a y-intercept of <m>(0,2)</m>, which we can use to graph the equation quickly.
    </p>
     <image width="60%">
      <shortdescription>a graph of a y = -x+2</shortdescription>
        <prefigure label="pf-101ex2a" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
        <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="5">
        <definition>f(x) = -x+2 </definition>
                <coordinates bbox="(-6,-6,6,6)">
        <grid-axes arrows="2" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"
        hticks="(-5,1,5)" vticks="(-5,1,5)" hlabels="(-5,1,5)" vlabels="(-5,1,5)"/>
                           <graph function = "f" stroke = "red"/>
                      </coordinates>
        </diagram>
        </prefigure>
        </image>
<p>
  The second equation is also not written in slope-intercept form, so we start by solving for  <m>y</m>:
  <md>
    <mrow>x-2y \amp =4</mrow>
    <mrow>x-2y -x\amp =4-x</mrow>
    <mrow>2y \amp =4-x</mrow>
    <mrow>\frac{-2y}{-2}\amp =\frac{4-x}{2}</mrow>
    <mrow>y \amp \frac{4}{-2} - \frac{x}{-2}</mrow>
    <mrow>y \amp =-2+x</mrow>
    <mrow>y \amp =x-2</mrow>
  </md>
  Now we can see that the second equation has a slope of <m>m=1</m> and a y-intercept of <m>(0,-1)</m>, which we can use to graph the equation quickly:
</p>
        <image width="60%">
      <shortdescription>a graph of a y = -x+2 and y = x-2</shortdescription>
        <prefigure label="pf-101ex2b" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
        <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="5">
        <definition>f(x) = -x+2 </definition>
        <definition>g(x) = x-2 </definition>
        <coordinates bbox="(-6,-6,6,6)">
        <grid-axes arrows="2" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"
        hticks="(-5,1,5)" vticks="(-5,1,5)" hlabels="(-5,1,5)" vlabels="(-5,1,5)"/>
             <graph function = "g" stroke = "blue"/>
              <graph function = "f" stroke = "red"/>
              <point p="(2,0)" stroke="black" fill="black"/>
                <label anchor="(2,-1)" alignment="south">
                <m>(2, 0)</m>
                </label>
        </coordinates>
        </diagram>
        </prefigure>
        </image>
        <p>
          Notice that the point <m>(x,y)=(2,0)</m> lies on both lines; it is the point where the two lines intersect. So, <m>x=2,y=0</m> is the solution to the system.
        </p>
  </example>
  <worksheet xml:id="ws-s10e01">
    <title>Season 10, Episode 1 Practice Problems</title>
    <exercise>
      <introduction>
        <p>
          Check to see if the given point is a solution to the following systems of equations
        </p>
      </introduction>
      <task workspace="0.75in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <md>
        \left\{ \begin{array}{l}x+3y = -9\\ 2x-4y = 12 \end{array}\right., \quad (-3,-2)
            </md>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task workspace="0.75in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <md>
        \left\{ \begin{array}{l} x+3y  = -9\\ 2x-4y  = 12 \end{array}\right., \quad (0,-3)
            </md>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task workspace="0.75in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <md>
        \left\{ \begin{array}{l} x+y  = 8\\ y  = x-4 \end{array}\right., \quad (6,-2)
            </md>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task workspace="0.75in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <md>
        \left\{ \begin{array}{l} x+y  = 8\\ y  = x-4 \end{array}\right., \quad (9,-1)
            </md>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
    </exercise>
    <exercise>
      <introduction>
        <p>
          Solve the following systems by graphing.
        </p>
      </introduction>
      <task workspace="2.5in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <md>
        \left\{ \begin{array}{l} 3x+y  = 6\\ x+3y  = -6 \end{array}\right.
        </md>
          </p>
          <image width="80%">
      <shortdescription>blank xy-plane</shortdescription>
        <prefigure label="pf-101x1a" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
        <diagram dimensions="(500,500)" margins="5">
        <coordinates bbox="(-6,-6,6,6)">
        <grid-axes arrows="2" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"
        hticks="(-5,1,5)" vticks="(-5,1,5)" hlabels="(-5,1,5)" vlabels="(-5,1,5)"/>
        </coordinates>
        </diagram>
        </prefigure>
        </image>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task workspace="2.5in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <md>
        \left\{ \begin{array}{l} y  = x-2\\ y  = -2x-2 \end{array}\right.
            </md>
          </p>
          <image width="80%">
      <shortdescription>blank xy-plane</shortdescription>
        <prefigure label="pf-101x1b" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
        <diagram dimensions="(500,500)" margins="5">
        <coordinates bbox="(-6,-6,6,6)">
        <grid-axes arrows="2" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"
        hticks="(-5,1,5)" vticks="(-5,1,5)" hlabels="(-5,1,5)" vlabels="(-5,1,5)"/>
        </coordinates>
        </diagram>
        </prefigure>
        </image>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task workspace="2.5in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <md>
        \left\{ \begin{array}{l} 2x-y = 6\\ y  = 4 \end{array}\right.
            </md>
          </p>
          <image width="80%">
      <shortdescription>blank xy-plane</shortdescription>
        <prefigure label="pf-101x1c" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
        <diagram dimensions="(500,500)" margins="5">
        <coordinates bbox="(-6,-6,6,6)">
        <grid-axes arrows="2" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"
        hticks="(-5,1,5)" vticks="(-5,1,5)" hlabels="(-5,1,5)" vlabels="(-5,1,5)"/>
        </coordinates>
        </diagram>
        </prefigure>
        </image>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task workspace="2.5in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <md>
        \left\{ \begin{array}{l} x+4y  = -1\\ x  = 3 \end{array}\right.
            </md>
          </p>
          <image width="80%">
      <shortdescription>blank xy-plane</shortdescription>
        <prefigure label="pf-101x1d" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
        <diagram dimensions="(500,500)" margins="5">
        <coordinates bbox="(-6,-6,6,6)">
        <grid-axes arrows="2" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"
        hticks="(-5,1,5)" vticks="(-5,1,5)" hlabels="(-5,1,5)" vlabels="(-5,1,5)"/>
        </coordinates>
        </diagram>
        </prefigure>
        </image>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <md>
        \left\{ \begin{array}{l} -x+2y  = 4\\ y = \frac{1}{2}x-3 \end{array}\right.
            </md>
          </p>
          <image width="80%">
      <shortdescription>blank xy-plane</shortdescription>
        <prefigure label="pf-101x1e" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
        <diagram dimensions="(500,500)" margins="5">
        <coordinates bbox="(-6,-6,6,6)">
        <grid-axes arrows="2" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"
        hticks="(-5,1,5)" vticks="(-5,1,5)" hlabels="(-5,1,5)" vlabels="(-5,1,5)"/>
        </coordinates>
        </diagram>
        </prefigure>
        </image>
        </statement>
      </task>
    </exercise>
  </worksheet>
</section>  